{"crc":"ff96f26d","kind":"Candidate","payload":{"response":"The issue is that when a URL pattern has an optional named group, the `RegexPattern.match()` method in `django/urls/resolvers.py` filters out `None` values from the kwargs, but then passes the remaining groups as positional args. This causes the view to receive both positional and keyword arguments for the same parameter.\n\nThe fix is to ensure that when there are named groups (even if some are `None`), we don't fall back to positional arguments. We should only use positional arguments when there are no named groups at all.\n\nHere's the fix:\n\n# path: django/urls/resolvers.py\n<<<<<<< SEARCH\n    def match(self, path):\n        match = self.regex.search(path)\n        if match:\n            # If there are any named groups, use those as kwargs, ignoring\n            # non-named groups. Otherwise, pass all non-named arguments as\n            # positional arguments.\n            kwargs = {k: v for k, v in match.groupdict().items() if v is not None}\n            args = () if kwargs else match.groups()\n            return path[match.end():], args, kwargs\n        return None\n=======\n    def match(self, path):\n        match = self.regex.search(path)\n        if match:\n            # If there are any named groups, use those as kwargs, ignoring\n            # non-named groups. Otherwise, pass all non-named arguments as\n            # positional arguments.\n            kwargs = match.groupdict()\n            args = () if kwargs else match.groups()\n            return path[match.end():], args, kwargs\n        return None\n=======\n    def match(self, path):\n        match = self.regex.search(path)\n        if match:\n            # If there are any named groups, use those as kwargs, ignoring\n            # non-named groups. Otherwise, pass all non-named arguments as\n            # positional arguments.\n            kwargs = match.groupdict()\n            args = () if kwargs else match.groups()\n            retur